28. Price calculation engine
This is more complicated than displaying a daily rate.
A proper pricing engine should support configurable rules.
Potential factors:
•	Vehicle daily rate.
•	Hourly rate.
•	Rental duration.
•	Weekend pricing.
•	Seasonal pricing.
•	Long-term rental discounts.
•	Chauffeur charges.
•	Distance-based charges.
•	Airport transfer fees.
•	Additional equipment.
•	Additional driver.
•	Taxes.
•	Security deposit.
•	Late return fees.
Example:
\[ \text{Total} = \text{Rental} + \text{Services} + \text{Applicable Charges} \]
Security deposits must be displayed separately from the rental revenue.
Price transparency requirement
The customer should see the estimated total before submitting a booking.
If a price cannot be calculated automatically, show:
"Price requires company confirmation."
Never invent a final price.
29. Availability engine
This is arguably the most important technical dependency of the storefront.
The proposal specifically requires a backend check against actual physical vehicle inventory and overlapping dates. 
Kigali Fleet Management SaaS Proposal.pdf
The storefront needs to consider:
•	Existing confirmed reservations.
•	Temporary reservation holds.
•	Maintenance periods.
•	Vehicle status.
•	Rental return buffers.
•	Driver availability for chauffeur services.
•	Booking approval requirements.
Temporary reservation holds
Suppose a customer selects a vehicle and proceeds to payment.
Another customer tries to reserve the same vehicle.
The system needs a controlled temporary hold with an expiration time, where the booking flow requires it.
The hold must be released if:
•	Payment fails.
•	The customer abandons checkout.
•	The reservation expires.
•	The company rejects the request.
A hold must not become a permanent booking by accident.
For a vehicle with multiple overlapping inquiries, the backend must be the final authority, not the browser.
